at knew or  should have known that this was breaking  the law when to head anywhere  effectively well to issue an fpn  uh you would need strong evidence that  a breach had occurred  the criteria about that i just described  relate to opening the investigation at  the start  an assistant commissioner your criteria  for launching the investigation as you  said was that not doing so would  significantly undermine the legitimacy  of the law  in your eyes issuing these fines to  those people who made the laws  in itself has undermined the legitimacy  of those laws do you agree with that  that's not for me to comment on the  things that i  am here to tell you about is the  how we went about our investigation  uh and the conclusions that we have  reached  were you surprised about the level of  fixed penalty notices given that these  people did write the laws because i i  was surprised are you  i think a number of people members of  the public have been both surprised and  concerned  uh what they have heard and i'm sure  they will be surprised and concerned  about the outcome of our investigation  but we followed of you know a very  thorough impartial investigation  we've looked at over 1 000 documents  which included a whole range of  different material  and we've carried out a very careful  assessment process and that's reached  the conclusions that it's reached and  assistant chris you said at the  beginning that these were extraordinary  times the pandemic an unprecedented  situation and we've ended up in this  place have you ever done anything like  this before in terms of an investigation  that  has hit so closely on the people that  actually make laws  we have carried out various  investigations in the past into all  sorts of different people and the  metropolitan police like all police  services will always  uh if we're called on we'll always do  those sorts of investigations and we'll  do them impartially and just finally the  investigation has cost 460 000 pounds 